---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';

const locale = 'en';
const title = 'US EIN Format Validator | Check and Validate';
const description =
  'Free US EIN format validator. Check that an Employer Identification Number has the NN-NNNNNNN format and a valid IRS prefix, and learn how to find your EIN.';
const summary =
  'Enter a US Employer Identification Number (EIN) to check that it has nine digits in the NN-NNNNNNN format and a valid IRS prefix. This checks the format only. It does not confirm the EIN is registered to a business.';

const strings = {
  validHeadline: 'Valid EIN format',
  invalidHeadline: 'Not a valid EIN format',
  validNote: 'The number has nine digits and a prefix the IRS uses. This confirms the format, not that the EIN is registered.',
  err: {
    empty: 'Enter an EIN to check.',
    format: 'An EIN has exactly nine digits, written NN-NNNNNNN. Enter digits only.',
    prefix: 'The prefix {prefix} is not one the IRS assigns, so this is not a valid EIN format.',
  },
  labelFormatted: 'Formatted',
  labelPrefix: 'IRS prefix',
};

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between an EIN, an SSN, and a TIN?',
    answer:
      'TIN, or Taxpayer Identification Number, is the umbrella term. An EIN is the TIN for a business. An SSN is the TIN for an individual. All three are nine digits, but an EIN is written NN-NNNNNNN while an SSN is written NNN-NN-NNNN.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is an EIN public information?',
    answer:
      'Often, yes. Unlike an SSN, a business EIN is not treated as a secret. It appears on public filings, W-9 forms, and business documents, and non-profit EINs are searchable in IRS tax-exempt records. It should still be handled with care.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I verify another company\'s EIN with this tool?',
    answer:
      'Only its format. This tool confirms a number is shaped like a real EIN and uses a valid IRS prefix. It cannot tell you which business an EIN belongs to, because there is no public master lookup of every EIN.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What makes an EIN format invalid?',
    answer:
      'Two things: the wrong number of digits, or a first two digits that the IRS does not use. The IRS assigns EINs from a specific set of prefixes tied to the campus or method that issued them, so a prefix outside that set cannot be a real EIN.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is my EIN sent anywhere when I check it?',
    answer:
      'No. The check runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type is transmitted, stored, or logged.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout title={title} description={description} locale={locale} toolId="ein" summary={summary} faq={faq}>
  <Fragment slot="tool">
    <form class="calc-box" id="ein-form" novalidate>
      <div class="field" id="ein-field">
        <label for="ein-input">EIN</label>
        <span class="hint">Nine digits, with or without the dash.</span>
        <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="ein-input" class="mono" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="12-3456789" />
        <span class="error-msg" id="ein-error"></span>
      </div>
      <button type="submit" class="btn">Validate EIN</button>
    </form>

    <div class="result-box" id="result-box" hidden>
      <div class="result-headline" id="result-headline"></div>
      <p class="result-note" id="result-note"></p>
      <ul class="info-list" id="result-info"></ul>
    </div>

    <script type="application/json" id="tool-i18n" set:html={JSON.stringify(strings)}></script>
    <script>
      import '../../scripts/ein-ui.js';
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="body">
    <h2>How It Works</h2>
    <p>
      An Employer Identification Number is the tax ID the IRS gives a business, the way a Social
      Security Number identifies a person. It is nine digits, written with a dash after the first
      two, as NN-NNNNNNN.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Part</th><th>Digits</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Prefix</td><td>2</td><td>The IRS campus or method (such as the online application) that assigned the EIN</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Serial</td><td>7</td><td>The rest of the number, unique within that prefix</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The tool checks that there are exactly nine digits and that the first two are a prefix the
      IRS actually uses. The IRS only issues EINs from a set list of prefixes, so a number with a
      prefix outside that list cannot be a genuine EIN. It does not, and cannot, confirm that the
      EIN is registered to a real business, because there is no public database of every issued
      EIN.
    </p>
    <h3>Worked Example</h3>
    <p>
      Take the sample number 12-3456789, which is made up rather than a real business EIN. The
      prefix is 12, which is on the IRS list, and there are nine digits in total, so it passes the
      format check. A number like 07-1234567 would fail, because 07 is not a prefix the IRS
      assigns.
    </p>

    <h2>How to Find Your EIN</h2>
    <p>
      If you have misplaced your EIN, you do not need to apply for a new one. It is recorded in
      several places you can usually reach quickly:
    </p>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>The IRS confirmation notice (CP 575) you received when the EIN was first issued. This is the original source.</li>
      <li>A previously filed business tax return. The EIN is printed at the top.</li>
      <li>The paperwork for a bank account, loan, or licence opened under the business, which almost always records the EIN.</li>
      <li>Older IRS notices or letters addressed to the business.</li>
      <li>If none of those work, call the IRS Business and Specialty Tax Line and, after they verify you are authorised, they can give it to you over the phone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When You Would Use This</h2>
    <p>
      Business owners, bookkeepers, and anyone filling in a W-9 or a tax form use this to confirm
      an EIN is written correctly before it goes on paperwork, since a transposed digit can hold
      up a filing. It is also a quick sanity check when you are copying an EIN from one document to
      another and want to be sure the format and prefix are right.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
